+      "source": [
+        "## Primal, dual, and automatic formulations\n",
+        "\n",
+        "`ActiveSetSolverParams.qpFormulation` selects `Automatic` (the default),\n",
+        "`Primal`, or `Dual`, independently of `qpSubproblemSolver` (`Sparse` by default,\n",
+        "or `Dense`). Automatic selects Dual for cold starts with inequalities, regardless\n",
+        "of the inequality-to-variable ratio. A converged C++ warm start retains its previous\n",
+        "formulation when the supplied point is feasible. A nonconverged warm start or an\n",
+        "infeasible supplied point selects Dual. Values supplied without a state are a cold\n",
+        "proximal center. Equality-only and fully fixed problems use Primal directly.\n",
+        "Selection happens once during setup; the policy is a heuristic, not a speed guarantee.\n",
+        "\n",
+        "Dual uses a nonnegative-multiplier active set inspired by\n",
+        "[Arnstr\u00f6m, Bemporad, and Axehill (2022)](https://cse.lab.imtlucca.it/~bemporad/publications/papers/ieeetac_daqp.pdf).\n",
+        "Dense projects permanent equalities by default. Set\n",
+        "`params.projectDenseQpEqualities = False` to keep the original coordinates\n",
+        "and enforce equalities in the factorization, for either Primal or Dual.\n",
+        "This option leaves Sparse unchanged. Disabling projection may avoid its setup\n",
+        "cost, but retains more variables in subsequent solves; benchmark both choices.\n",
+        "Dense preserves supplied square-root costs in either mode. For a positive-definite\n",
+        "Hessian-only objective without equalities, it uses cached Cholesky solves\n",
+        "when the native Hessian is sufficiently well conditioned. Other cases retain\n",
+        "the rank-aware square-root path. This changes no tolerances or proximal\n",
+        "weight. Sparse applies the\n",
+        "cached equality-constrained inverse without a dense nullspace or global inverse;\n",
+        "only the dual working system is dense. Its fixed-factorization cache is independent\n",
+        "of the primal-only `useIncrementalSparseQp` option.\n",
+        "\n",
+        "Dual bypasses phase one and `qpInitialization`. Supplied finite values provide\n",
+        "the proximal center and result shapes and may violate primal constraints. Without\n",
+        "supplied values it starts with a zero center, zero multipliers and no working rows.\n",
+        "C++ state warm starts reuse row masks and multipliers; absent multipliers start at\n",
+        "zero and inequality multipliers are projected onto the valid sign. Public\n",
+        "inequality multipliers keep GTSAM's existing nonpositive sign convention.\n",
+        "\n",
+        "The requested `regularization` is a recentered proximal weight, never a permanent\n",
+        "objective penalty. Dual requires invertible curvature after equality elimination.\n",
+        "With singular curvature and zero regularization, Automatic selects Primal and\n",
+        "explicit Dual throws an explanatory error. Numerical failures after selection do\n",
+        "not trigger a switch. Convergence is certified against the original QP; the shared\n",
+        "`maxIterations` budget counts dual working solves across proximal iterations.\n",
+        "The C++ result state's `qpFormulation` records the resolved formulation.\n",
+        "\n",
+        "`computeFeasibleStep()` always uses primal feasible descent, retaining its existing\n",
+        "initialization and feasibility requirements. LPs resolve Automatic to Primal and reject Dual.\n",
+        "\n",
+        "```python\n",
+        "params = gtsam.ActiveSetSolverParams()\n",
+        "params.qpFormulation = gtsam.ActiveSetSolverParams.QpFormulation.Automatic\n",
+        "params.qpSubproblemSolver = gtsam.ActiveSetSolverParams.QpSubproblemSolver.Sparse\n",
+        "result = gtsam.ActiveSetSolver(problem, params).optimize()\n",
+        "```\n"
+      ]
