+    "## Position types and wrapper names\n",
+    "\n",
+    "In C++, `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactor<Position>` accepts either a fixed-size Eigen vector (such as `Point3`) or `AugmentedPoint<N>`, with `N` obtained from `traits<Position>::dimension`. Both represent the same $N$-dimensional position, use the same squared-range residual, and support conventional nonlinear optimization.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Use a standard point when nonlinear optimization is sufficient. Use `AugmentedPoint<N>` when you also want to convert compatible factors into a QCQP, for example for subsequent semidefinite relaxation. **`AugmentedPoint<N>` stores only the $N$ Euclidean coordinates**; its QCQP traits construct the additional lifted coordinates when needed. Selecting it does not automatically invoke an SDP solver or certify a global solution. The type and its QCQP traits are defined in `gtsam_unstable/geometry/AugmentedPoint.h`.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The `gtsam_unstable` Python module exposes the 2D and 3D factor variants as `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactor2` and `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactor3` (NumPy position vectors), or `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactorAugmentedPoint2` and `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactorAugmentedPoint3` (`gtsam_unstable.AugmentedPoint2` / `gtsam_unstable.AugmentedPoint3` positions), respectively.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Use `gtsam_unstable.insertAugmentedPoint3(key, point, values)` to store an augmented point in a stable `gtsam.Values` container, and `gtsam_unstable.atAugmentedPoint3(key, values)` to retrieve it. The matching `updateAugmentedPoint2/3` and `insertOrAssignAugmentedPoint2/3` helpers update or replace values. The dimension-specific QCQP conversion helpers also live in `gtsam_unstable`. These helpers keep unstable types out of the stable `Values` wrapper."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "f5f6e07c",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## Prepare one known-landmark range measurement\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Each factor needs four inputs:\n",
+    "\n",
+    "1. the point key `k`;\n",
+    "2. a fixed world-frame landmark `wL`;\n",
+    "3. the measured range `R`; and\n",
+    "4. a noise model describing uncertainty in the squared-range residual.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The residual $e$ is a scalar, so the corresponding noise model is one-dimensional, with scalar standard deviation $\\sigma$ and covariance $\\sigma^2$.\n"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 3,
+   "id": "6e719445",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "k = 0\n",
+    "wL = np.array([2.0, 3.0, 6.0])\n",
+    "R = 7.1\n",
+    "\n",
+    "sigma = 0.1  # squared-range residual standard deviation [m^2]\n",
+    "model = gtsam.noiseModel.Isotropic.Sigma(1, sigma)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "ef50107e",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## Localization with `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactor`\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Add the factor to an ordinary nonlinear graph and insert an initial point estimate under the same key."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 4,
+   "id": "321197fb",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "graph = gtsam.NonlinearFactorGraph()\n",
+    "graph.add(\n",
+    "    gtsam_unstable.KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactor3(k, wL, R, model)\n",
+    ")\n",
+    "\n",
+    "wX_initial = np.array([-0.1, 0.2, -0.05])\n",
+    "initial_wXs = gtsam.Values()\n",
+    "initial_wXs.insert(k, wX_initial)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "7b454e9f",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## Build a well-constrained graph\n",
+    "\n",
+    "A single range measurement does not uniquely determine the point state. In 2D, it constrains the point to a circle centered at the known landmark; in 3D, it constrains the point to a sphere. In a real localization graph, add factors for several distinct known landmarks and, where appropriate, point priors or other factors. Each observation should carry its own `R` and noise model. Reusing the same point key connects all observations made from that point.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "A known landmark is fixed factor data, not a variable in `Values`."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "c5e7d02d",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## QCQP conversion with `AugmentedPoint<N>`\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The squared-range residual is quadratic in $x$, making its least-squares cost quartic in the original point coordinates. Merely using a standard `Point3` state therefore does not give the factor the QCQP representation required by GTSAM's conversion interface. `AugmentedPoint<N>` supplies the appropriate lifting and constraint traits without changing the $N$-dimensional nonlinear state.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "For QCQP conversion, introduce the $N+2$ dimensional lifted variable $X$:\n",
+    "\n",
+    "$$\n",
+    "X =\n",
+    "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
+    "h \\\\\n",
+    "x \\\\\n",
+    "u\n",
+    "\\end{bmatrix},\n",
+    "\\qquad h=1,\\quad u=x^\\top x\n",
+    "\\quad\\text{for the canonical lift}.\n",
+    "$$\n",
+    "\n",
+    "For a known landmark $L_i$ and measured range $d_i$, the squared-range residual becomes linear in the lifted variable:\n",
+    "\n",
+    "$$\n",
+    "e_i(X) = b_i X, \\qquad\n",
+    "b_i = \\begin{bmatrix} \\|L_i\\|^2-d_i^2 & -2L_i^\\top & 1 \\end{bmatrix}.\n",
+    "$$\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The whitened least-squares cost is then quadratic in $X$. Two quadratic equalities enforce a valid homogeneous lift:\n",
+    "\n",
+    "$$\n",
+    "E(X) = \\frac12 \\sum_i \\frac{(b_i X)^2}{\\sigma_i^2},\n",
+    "\\qquad x^\\top x-hu=0, \\qquad h^2=1.\n",
+    "$$\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The constraint permits $h=\\pm 1$; the original position is recovered as $x/h$. The lifted QCQP variable is **not** the five-dimensional (for $N=3$) tangent or stored representation of `AugmentedPoint3`. In C++, the QCQP-capable factor is `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactor<AugmentedPoint<N>>`; in Python, it is `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactorAugmentedPoint2/3`.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The following cell converts the same measurement and checks that its lifted cost matches the original nonlinear cost at the same position. It does not solve either optimization problem: the [localization example](../../../python/gtsam_unstable/examples/slam/KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeLocalizationExample.ipynb) shows nonlinear and QCQP solves, with an optional SDP relaxation.\n"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 5,
+   "id": "8864cd27",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "name": "stdout",
+     "output_type": "stream",
+     "text": [
+      "Lifted coordinates [h, x, u]: [ 1.     -0.1     0.2    -0.05    0.0525]\n",
+      "QCQP cost: 121.29031249999946\n"
+     ]
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "augmented_graph = gtsam.NonlinearFactorGraph()\n",
+    "augmented_graph.add(\n",
+    "    gtsam_unstable.KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactorAugmentedPoint3(k, wL, R, model)\n",
+    ")\n",
+    "augmented_initial = gtsam.Values()\n",
+    "gtsam_unstable.insertAugmentedPoint3(k, gtsam_unstable.AugmentedPoint3(wX_initial), augmented_initial)\n",
+    "\n",
+    "qcqp = gtsam.QcqpProblem(augmented_graph, 1)\n",
+    "lifted_initial = gtsam.Values()\n",
+    "gtsam_unstable.insertQcqpValue3(k, gtsam_unstable.atAugmentedPoint3(k, augmented_initial), lifted_initial)\n",
+    "cost, equality_violation, inequality_violation = qcqp.evaluate(lifted_initial)\n",
+    "np.testing.assert_allclose(cost, graph.error(initial_wXs))\n",
+    "np.testing.assert_allclose([equality_violation, inequality_violation], 0.0, atol=1e-12)\n",
+    "print(\"Lifted coordinates [h, x, u]:\", lifted_initial.atMatrix(k).ravel())\n",
+    "print(\"QCQP cost:\", cost)\n"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "32acc07c",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## Practical checklist\n",
+    "\n",
+    "- Use `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactor2/3` for point localization from ranges to fixed, known landmarks using `NonlinearFactorGraph`.\n",
+    "- Use `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactorAugmentedPoint2/3` when constructing a `QcqpProblem`; the augmented type represents the same point and also supports ordinary nonlinear optimization.\n",
+    "- QCQP conversion alone is not a global optimality guarantee; a separate solver and, where applicable, a tight SDP bound are needed.\n",
+    "- Express the state point and landmark in the same coordinate frame.\n",
+    "- Pass the ordinary non-negative measured range `R`; the factor squares it internally.\n",
+    "- Supply a one-dimensional noise model, since the squared-range residual is scalar.\n",
+    "- Interpret the noise model as uncertainty on the squared-range residual, not directly on the range measurement.\n",
+    "- Add measurements from enough geometrically distinct landmarks, or other factors, to fully constrain the point state.\n",
+    "- Keep known landmarks as fixed factor data; they are not variables in `Values`.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "## Related documentation\n",
+    "\n",
+    "- [KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeLocalizationExample](../../../python/gtsam_unstable/examples/slam/KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeLocalizationExample.ipynb) demonstrates 3D point localization from noisy range measurements to fixed known landmarks.\n",
+    "- [KnownLandmarkFactor](../../../gtsam/slam/doc/KnownLandmarkFactor.ipynb) documents Cartesian observations of fixed known landmarks using pose states."
+   ]
+  }
